static ORION_STUDIO_SC_PARAM_16_READBACK: ParamReference = ParamReference { text: "FOUND 2026-08-31 -- see frame.readback. It is category 0x03: one record per destination group, `<dest_id>` + a (bank,idx) pair per output channel, identical to the write frame. Verified byte-for-byte against routes the CLI wrote from Linux, and shown correctly by a fresh Windows Launcher (CAPTURE E'). `matrix-status` now does a live read. --- The text below is the SUPERSEDED pre-decode analysis (kept for the reasoning trail); its error was assuming the readback would be a HID Feature report / control transfer. It is neither -- it is an in-band 0x74-request / 0x75-response on the interrupt endpoints, and a bare 0x74 (no 10-00-00-00 sub-header) is ignored, which is why tools/readback_probe.py --poke drew a blank. --- NO USB READBACK -- as of 2026-08-31 this is close to a hard conclusion, not just 'not found'. The HID interface's report descriptor (54 bytes, dumped from Linux hidraw via tools/hid_probe.py: 06a0ff 0901 a101 0902 a100 06a1ff 0903 0904 1580257f350045ff 7508 964001 8102  0905 0906 ... 9102 c0 c0) declares EXACTLY one 320-byte Input report and one 320-byte Output report, NO Feature report, NO report IDs. The device STALLs (EPIPE) every GET_REPORT on the control pipe -- both GET_FEATURE and GET_REPORT(Input) at every length tried. So there is no HID-level readback path at all: the device only ever emits unsolicited 0x73/0x74/0x75 interrupt reports, and none of those carry routing (verified full-width, all 320 bytes, on the on2/on3 diff pair AND every matrix capture -- zero bytes track routing). The UAC2 config descriptor has no feature/selector/mixer units, so nothing routing-related is exposed there either. No vendor control request appears in ANY capture (connect, preset-LOAD, per-control). LOADING A PRESET is pure push: 0x53 routing frames bracketed by SET_GLOBAL(param 0x2c) value 1 then 0 (a batch/edit-lock marker), plus any gain frames -- the Launcher never queries the device, it writes the file's contents. CROSS-MACHINE PERSISTENCE (the original reason to think a readback existed): the device DOES store routing in NVRAM (front panel + survives power-cycle), but the cross-machine agreement the user saw is almost certainly the Antelope Launcher's own account/session sync (server-side, keyed to the logged-in account), NOT a device->host report. DECISIVE TEST STILL TO DO: change a route on the device's FRONT PANEL with no computer attached, then connect a fresh/offline Launcher -- if it shows the change there is a vendor request fired on routing-tab open (CAPTURE E', last stone); if it shows stale/default routing, there is definitively no device readback and the CLI cache is the correct and only design. CAPTURE E (macos-antelopeINIT-poweroff-on2/on3): ruled out the connect handshake -- entire host->device init is one frame SET_PARAM(0x49,ch1,0); 0x73/0x74/descriptors byte-identical between the two routings bar a preamp-gain byte + meter noise.", formula: "", offsets: &[] };
